# Cloudflare Pages serves these with the PWA (web.ringdrill.app).
#
# Why: the web build is `flutter build web --wasm`, and skwasm renders on
# several threads only when the page is *cross-origin isolated*. Without these
# two headers it silently drops to a single thread, which is what the console
# has been saying on every load.
#
# Isolation needs COOP `same-origin` plus a COEP value, and the choice of COEP
# is the whole risk:
#
#   require-corp   every cross-origin subresource must send CORP. The map
#                  tiles do not — cache.kartverket.no answers with
#                  `access-control-allow-origin: *` and no CORP header — so
#                  this would blank the map, which is most of what this app is.
#   credentialless what is used here. Cross-origin no-cors requests are allowed
#                  and sent without credentials. The tiles need no credentials,
#                  so nothing is lost, and CanvasKit keeps loading from gstatic
#                  (which does send CORP: cross-origin either way).
#
# A browser that does not know `credentialless` ignores it, is not isolated,
# and runs single-threaded — exactly today's behaviour, so old Safari is no
# worse off than it is now.
#
# **The one thing this breaks, when it arrives.** COOP `same-origin` severs
# `window.opener`, and provider sign-in on web goes through
# `flutter_web_auth_2`, which is a popup that reports back to its opener. That
# is inert today because no OAuth providers are configured — /api/auth/providers
# answers with an empty list — but the day Google or Microsoft is enabled, this
# file and that flow are in direct conflict. `same-origin-allow-popups` would
# fix the popup and forfeit isolation: it is not one of the values that grants
# it. The way out is a redirect flow rather than a popup, which the backend
# already supports (`/api/auth/callback/<provider>` bounces to the app).
/*
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: credentialless
